  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$ of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=10-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $10-2y>0$ hence $y<5.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(10-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-40{y}^{2}+100y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-80y+100,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-80y+100 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-20y+25 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $5$ since we already know that $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-80.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})=-40$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume.",
